@startuml StateSync
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title State sync — moving resume state between the operator's home and a\nsandbox-local volume without ever leaving a partial file

node "host" as HOST <<host>> {
  database "PROVEO_HOME/.claude\nprojects · sessions · todos\nthe operator's ONLY copy" as MOUNT <<db>>
}

node "sandbox" as BOX <<app>> {
  component "proveo_seed\nkit setup.startup" as SEED <<app>>
  component "proveo_sync_state restore\nhost → volume" as RESTORE <<app>>
  component "proveo_sync_state save\nvolume → host" as SAVE <<async>>
  component "_proveo_sync_lock\nmkdir, pid-checked" as LOCK <<app>>
  component "_proveo_sync_tree\n1. cp -an  (new files, no clobber)\n2. temp + rename  (differing files)" as TREE <<app>>
  database "sandbox-local volume\n$HOME/.claude/*\ndies with `sbx rm`" as VOL <<db>>
}

component "proveo teardown + failure path\nSaveState → sbx exec -w /" as FAIL <<error>>

SEED ARROW_MAIN RESTORE : before the agent looks for transcripts
FAIL ARROW_ERROR SAVE : on a STOPPED sandbox this STARTS the VM…
FAIL ARROW_ERROR SEED : …which re-runs the seed
RESTORE ARROW_MAIN LOCK
SAVE ARROW_MAIN LOCK
LOCK ARROW_MAIN TREE : one direction at a time
TREE ARROW_MAIN VOL : restore
TREE ARROW_QUEUE MOUNT : save
MOUNT ARROW_OPTIONAL RESTORE : yesterday's sessions

note right of FAIL
  **The collision, measured.** The failure handler copies state out before it
  looks for a transcript, and on a stopped sandbox ""sbx exec"" starts the VM to
  do it — so the kit's startup seed is still inside ""restore"" when the exec's
  own ""save"" begins. Two copies, opposite directions, same files.

  Mounts re-evaluated **15:12:13.030**; the colliding writes landed
  **15:12:13.62–.69**. Seven of the operator's transcripts were rewritten short
  in that one second — 7340032, 786432, 786432, 524288, 262144, 262144 and
  262144 bytes, //every size an exact 256 KiB multiple//, each cut mid-JSON —
  and the short copy was then propagated to the other side, so no intact copy
  remained. Nothing reported it: the copy sent stderr to ""/dev/null"" and ended
  in ""|| true"".
end note

note left of SAVE
  **""-w /"", or the save never runs.** ""sbx exec"" starts in the container's
  WorkingDir — the first workspace — and on this backend that directory can
  stop resolving while the sandbox lives on (virtiofs-cwd-invalidation.puml).
  The runtime then dies at chdir, ""OCI runtime exec failed: getcwd: Operation
  not permitted"", exit 127, before the sync runs a line; teardown reported
  "resume state not preserved" and carried on to ""sbx rm"". Four days of
  transcripts on proveo-1787956302-22788 went with the volumes.

  The sync reads nothing from the cwd: the lib is sourced by absolute path and
  every directory it moves comes from ""$HOME"" and ""PROVEO_STATE_HOME"", so ""/""
  costs nothing and is the one directory every container has.
end note

note bottom of TREE
  **Why two phases and not one ""cp -a"".** ""cp"" opens its destination with
  ""O_TRUNC"" and streams into it, so an interrupted or concurrent copy leaves the
  destination cut at a read-buffer boundary. Atomicity is only needed where there
  is something to destroy, so the work splits:

  * **phase 1** — one ""cp -an"" places every file the destination does NOT have.
    No existing file is opened for writing, so this phase cannot truncate.
  * **phase 2** — files present on BOTH sides and differing are copied to a temp
    name in the destination directory and //renamed// over the target.
    ""rename(2)"" is atomic: a reader sees the old file or the new one, never a
    short one, and an interruption leaves debris instead of damage.

  Files only the destination has are left alone. A sync has never deleted, and
  the operator's home holds sessions this sandbox was never given.
end note

note bottom of LOCK
  **""mkdir"", not ""flock"".** Atomic on every filesystem in play and needs no
  util-linux in the image. Both callers live in one PID namespace, so the pid
  recorded in the lock settles whether a holder is alive — a seed killed
  mid-copy leaves debris, and debris that blocked every later run forever would
  be a worse failure than the one being fixed. Waiting caps at 60s, an order of
  magnitude over any measured sync.
end note

note as Stamps #F4F9FB
  **The skip, and the one case where a stamp lies.**

  Phase 2 must not fork per file for a tree that has not changed: measured in
  the sandbox image, 5000 shared files cost **4.6s** through a fork-per-file
  copy against **0.067s** for one bulk ""cp -a"", and ""restore"" is already racing
  the agent's own start. ""cp -a"" preserves size and mtime, so equal size plus
  equal mtime is normally the copy having already happened.

  //Normally is not always.// On the sandbox's overlay filesystem two files
  written in the same clock tick report the SAME nanosecond mtime — measured,
  both sides ""1787846280.6672317090"", same size, different content. A stamp is
  therefore trusted as an identity only once it has stopped being "now":
  anything modified inside a 5s grace window is copied whatever its stamps say.

  That is exactly the set that matters. On a ""save"" those are the transcripts
  the agent just wrote; on a ""restore"" there are none.
end note

note right of VOL
  **Why any of this exists.** sbx mounts ""$HOME/.claude/projects"", ""sessions"",
  ""todos"" and friends as PER-SANDBOX volumes, and ""sbx rm --force"" removes
  "VM + images + volumes" — so without a copy-out, ""--resume"" loses every
  session the moment the run ends. The dirs are discovered from ""/proc/mounts""
  rather than tabulated per agent: a block device under ""$HOME"" is by
  definition sandbox-local, and a hand-written table goes stale the moment sbx
  adds one. ""statsig"" and ""shell-snapshots"" are skipped — telemetry and
  scratch, never read back.
end note
@enduml
